<title>USA: Boeing &quot;disappointed&quot; but still seeks EU's ok.</title>
<headline>Boeing &quot;disappointed&quot; but still seeks EU's ok.</headline>
<dateline>SEATTLE 1997-07-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>A Boeing Co spokeswoman said on Friday that the aircraft manufacturer was &quot;disappointed&quot; with the European Union's threat to block a proposed merger between Boeing and McDonnell Douglas but the company would continue to work toward an acceptable agreement.</p>
<p>&quot;Understandably, we are disappointed, but we are committed to continuing to work with the European Commission to reach a positive outcome,&quot; said Boeing spokesman Sherry Nebel. &quot;We are optimistic that there is time to work out these issues.&quot;</p>
<p>She added that Boeing and McDonnell &quot;still have until July 23 to continue our efforts.&quot;</p>
<p>The EU is to announce its final decision on the merger on that date.</p>
<p>EU officials meeting in Brussels today said the merger plan in its current state was &quot;not sufficient&quot; under EU antitrust law and threatened to block it if changes were not made.</p>
</text>
